{
  "concepts": [
    {
      "conditions": [],
      "definition": "The single core result domain for every performed evaluation, including constraints, judgments, frame evaluations, stops, work steps and effects directly or through mapped refinements: satisfied means the declared predicate holds on the exact basis; falsified means a declared counterexample defeats it; indeterminate means available admissible evidence cannot decide; out_of_frame means a required material relation or capability is undeclared; invalid_basis means the subject or basis cannot support evaluation.",
      "exceptions": [],
      "grounding": [
        {
          "end_line": 801,
          "quote": "| `satisfied` | the declared predicate holds on the exact basis |\n| `falsified` | a declared counterexample defeats it |\n| `indeterminate` | the available admissible evidence cannot decide it |\n| `out_of_frame` | the evaluation requires an undeclared material relation or capability |\n| `invalid_basis` | the subject or basis cannot support the evaluation |",
          "section_id": "repo://stdo-v3-generation-rc9/claude/c2cfa9b908d1722ae3f26c32fa4ece691083bdc3/source/standards/FOUNDATIONS.md#FOUNDATIONS%2F4.%20Algebra%2FResults",
          "start_line": 797
        }
      ],
      "key": "core_result_algebra",
      "label": "Core result algebra"
    },
    {
      "conditions": [],
      "definition": "A finer domain of outcomes defined by a member, profile or adopting Product, naming its question and mapped predicate. Further refinements may split parent values by cause, including refinements by projects. Domain meaning belongs to its definer, not to the core mapping.",
      "exceptions": [],
      "grounding": [
        {
          "end_line": 871,
          "quote": "Refinements. A member, profile or adopting Product that needs finer\noutcomes defines a refinement. The refinement names the question it\nanswers and the predicate its mapping is for, and maps every refined value\nto exactly one core value. No outcome exists outside the core; a\nrefinement adds no core value and keeps the pressure of `indeterminate`,\n`out_of_frame` and `invalid_basis` explicit. Where a refined value would\nmap to one of two core values according to its cause, the cause is part of\nthe refined value and each resulting value maps once (P5, P1). A further\nrefinement, by a member, profile, project or adopting Product, may split a\nrefined value by cause. The parent-value rule governs every such split:",
          "section_id": "repo://stdo-v3-generation-rc9/claude/c2cfa9b908d1722ae3f26c32fa4ece691083bdc3/source/standards/FOUNDATIONS.md#FOUNDATIONS%2F4.%20Algebra%2FResults",
          "start_line": 862
        }
      ],
      "key": "result_refinement",
      "label": "Result refinement"
    },
    {
      "conditions": ["An evaluation was required or planned but not performed."],
      "definition": "Absence of a closed result, distinct from every core value, including indeterminate. A missing result enters conjunction as a planned assessment without an applicable closed result when planned, or as uncovered required coverage when no assessment addresses the obligation.",
      "exceptions": [],
      "grounding": [
        {
          "end_line": 847,
          "quote": "Not performed. An evaluation that is required or planned and was not\nperformed has no closed result; an actor cannot report an evaluation it\ndid not perform (P6). Nothing is coerced to success: a failed,\nindeterminate, out-of-frame, out-of-scope or invalid-basis result follows\nthe declared result algebra and returns its own value under it, never\n`satisfied`, and a missing result, being no result, enters every\nconjunction over it as a gap: a planned assessment without a closed result\nwhere an assessment was planned, and uncovered coverage where none was.",
          "section_id": "repo://stdo-v3-generation-rc9/claude/c2cfa9b908d1722ae3f26c32fa4ece691083bdc3/source/standards/FOUNDATIONS.md#FOUNDATIONS%2F4.%20Algebra%2FResults",
          "start_line": 840
        }
      ],
      "key": "evaluation_nonperformance",
      "label": "Evaluation not performed"
    },
    {
      "conditions": [],
      "definition": "Three separate result predicates: availability asks whether a step produced a candidate; assessment asks whether the candidate meets declared criteria; acceptance asks whether the decision owner accepts it at its boundary. A step's stop state answers a further, separate question.",
      "exceptions": [],
      "grounding": [
        {
          "end_line": 891,
          "quote": "Availability, assessment and acceptance are separate results, each with its\nown predicate: that a step produced a candidate; that the candidate meets\nits declared criteria; and that the\n[decision owner](ACTIVATION.md#roles-and-delegation) accepts it at its\nboundary. A step having produced a candidate never maps to the candidate's\nfitness or acceptance, and acceptance is never inferred from a successful\nstep (P6; [ACTIVATION §3](ACTIVATION.md#3-step-results-and-disposition),",
          "section_id": "repo://stdo-v3-generation-rc9/claude/c2cfa9b908d1722ae3f26c32fa4ece691083bdc3/source/standards/FOUNDATIONS.md#FOUNDATIONS%2F4.%20Algebra%2FResults",
          "start_line": 885
        },
        {
          "end_line": 939,
          "quote": "The evaluation's result and the step's stop state answer different\nquestions, so the step's stop value never becomes the evaluation's\nresult.",
          "section_id": "repo://stdo-v3-generation-rc9/claude/c2cfa9b908d1722ae3f26c32fa4ece691083bdc3/source/standards/FOUNDATIONS.md#FOUNDATIONS%2F4.%20Algebra%2FResults",
          "start_line": 937
        }
      ],
      "key": "candidate_result_predicates",
      "label": "Availability, assessment and acceptance"
    },
    {
      "conditions": [],
      "definition": "Combination of results into one result for a composite predicate: component claims, a decision over evaluations, or the outer contract of composed steps. Its three distinct inputs are returned closed results with predicates and domain outcomes; planned assessments lacking an applicable closed result; and required coverage R minus coverage C admitted by planned assessments.",
      "exceptions": [],
      "grounding": [
        {
          "end_line": 1014,
          "quote": "Conjunction combines results into one result for a composite predicate: a\nclaim made of component claims, a decision over several evaluations, or\nthe outer contract of composed steps. It is defined here once, over the\ncore. A member or profile that combines results applies this rule and\ndeclares only the choices it leaves open (P4a, P4b, P5, P6).",
          "section_id": "repo://stdo-v3-generation-rc9/claude/c2cfa9b908d1722ae3f26c32fa4ece691083bdc3/source/standards/FOUNDATIONS.md#FOUNDATIONS%2F4.%20Algebra%2FConjunction%20of%20results",
          "start_line": 1010
        },
        {
          "end_line": 1030,
          "quote": "1. the closed results returned, each a core value with its predicate and\n   domain outcome;\n2. the planned assessments that have no closed result applicable to the\n   composite: evaluations the composite requires that were not performed,\n   or whose result is absent, stale, bound to another subject or basis, or\n   incapable of establishing its claim;\n3. the required coverage that no planned assessment addresses: for a\n   required obligation set R and the coverage C that the planned\n   assessments admit, the uncovered coverage R minus C.",
          "section_id": "repo://stdo-v3-generation-rc9/claude/c2cfa9b908d1722ae3f26c32fa4ece691083bdc3/source/standards/FOUNDATIONS.md#FOUNDATIONS%2F4.%20Algebra%2FConjunction%20of%20results",
          "start_line": 1022
        }
      ],
      "key": "result_conjunction",
      "label": "Conjunction of results"
    },
    {
      "conditions": [],
      "definition": "The declared decision policy of a conjunction: mandatory results, conflict and indeterminacy handling, required basis agreement or coherent composite basis, owning decision authority, treatment of nonmandatory results, and any veto or deferral.",
      "exceptions": [],
      "grounding": [
        {
          "end_line": 1118,
          "quote": "The declared rule. A conjunction declares which results are mandatory; how\nconflict and indeterminacy are handled; which basis relations must agree,\nresults on different bases combining only under a declared coherent\ncomposite basis (§2, Basis); which decision authority owns the\nconjunction; how results that are not mandatory count; and whether any\nresult may veto or defer the decision.\nConjunction does not transfer the semantic authority of a contributing\nevaluation (P6, P4a).",
          "section_id": "repo://stdo-v3-generation-rc9/claude/c2cfa9b908d1722ae3f26c32fa4ece691083bdc3/source/standards/FOUNDATIONS.md#FOUNDATIONS%2F4.%20Algebra%2FConjunction%20of%20results",
          "start_line": 1111
        }
      ],
      "key": "declared_conjunction_rule",
      "label": "Declared conjunction rule"
    },
    {
      "conditions": [
        "Instance premises are supplied by the situation, not by the commitments: an evaluation declares predicate, subject and basis; evaluations, steps or members are combined into a decision; or finer outcomes are needed, as applicable."
      ],
      "definition": "Family F3 records eighteen foundation-to-law traces for results and combination. It distinguishes independent P1, P2, P3, P4a and P6, recovered P0, P5 derived from P0/P6, and P4b derived from P0/P2; identifies added policy choices and their owners; and indexes consumers without giving them additional deciding scope. Its traces cover the core, standing, recording, nonperformance, refinement, candidate predicates, frame boundary, imports, conjunction inputs, stale results, reported value, decision rule, composition, conservation accounting, reference adequacy, latitude, shared machinery and categorical demonstration.",
      "exceptions": [],
      "grounding": [
        {
          "end_line": 951,
          "quote": "Foundation to law for results and their combination (family F3). The\ntable covers every rule of §4 and the result grounds of §2 Imports\nbetween members, in the format of\n[Foundation to law](#foundation-to-law), which states the standing of its\nconsequences. Purpose and material relation: a reader of §4 sees which\ncommitments each result rule rests on and which choices it adds, with their\nowners (P6, P2). Instance premises, which the commitments do not supply:\n(i) an evaluation declares its predicate, subject and basis; (ii) results\nof several evaluations, steps or members are combined into one decision;\n(iii) members, profiles and adopting Products need outcomes finer than the\ncore.",
          "section_id": "repo://stdo-v3-generation-rc9/claude/c2cfa9b908d1722ae3f26c32fa4ece691083bdc3/source/standards/FOUNDATIONS.md#FOUNDATIONS%2F4.%20Algebra%2FResults",
          "start_line": 941
        }
      ],
      "key": "result_foundation_law_trace",
      "label": "Result foundation-to-law family"
    },
    {
      "conditions": [
        "Applicable instance premises: construction with intake-bound governing basis and material assumptions Γ; declared domain Ω of cases including material inputs, behaviour and environment; checker with declared observations and admitted evaluation relation."
      ],
      "definition": "Shared source owner connecting construction to assertions, observations and evidence for preservation, assertion, provenance, quantified assurance, reuse and formal representation. The cited ACTIVATION, EVALUATION, ASSURANCE, RELEASE, SPEC_METHOD and a_c surfaces apply these relations without stating a second relation. The notation states proof obligations rather than discharging them.",
      "exceptions": [],
      "grounding": [
        {
          "end_line": 1149,
          "quote": "The relations below connect construction to the assertions, observations\nand evidence that support claims about it. They are the shared owner for\nthe preservation, assertion, provenance, quantified-assurance, reuse and\nformal-representation obligations in this corpus; ACTIVATION §1, §4, §5 and §7, EVALUATION §1 and §3,\nASSURANCE §3, §10, §11 and §14, RELEASE §8 and §9, SPEC_METHOD §4 and\nthe a_c interpretation apply them and state no second relation (P6). They are law\ncarried from the common assurance requirement. A tenant chooses its\nrepresentation and execution within these obligations. The notation\nstates proof obligations; writing an implication does not discharge it.\n\nFoundation to law. This family, assurance relations, covers the six\nsubsections that follow. Its instance premises are: (a) a construction\nwith the governing basis bound at intake under ACTIVATION §1 and the\nmaterial assumptions, together written Γ here; (b) a declared domain Ω of cases, each with the inputs,\nbehaviour and environment material to an obligation; (c) a checker with\ndeclared observations and an admitted evaluation relation. They are\nconditions of the situations the family governs, not commitments.",
          "section_id": "repo://stdo-v3-generation-rc9/claude/c2cfa9b908d1722ae3f26c32fa4ece691083bdc3/source/standards/FOUNDATIONS.md#FOUNDATIONS%2F4.%20Algebra%2FAssurance%20relations",
          "start_line": 1133
        }
      ],
      "key": "assurance_relations",
      "label": "Assurance relations"
    },
    {
      "conditions": [],
      "definition": "Construction binds starting state A and required outcome Z, including material required effects and trace properties, under governing basis and assumptions Γ. At boundary i, X_i is a state or trace domain, F_i ⊆ X_i × X_(i+1) the transition relation, and P_i the property retained or established. Claimed preservation is the universal obligation: for all x,x', P_i(x) and F_i(x,x') implies P_(i+1)(x').",
      "exceptions": [],
      "grounding": [
        {
          "end_line": 1192,
          "quote": "For a sequence of transformations, let `X_i` be the state or trace domain\nat boundary i, `F_i ⊆ X_i × X_(i+1)` the declared transition relation, and\n`P_i` the property to retain or establish there. A claimed preservation\nstep has the form:\n\n```text\nFor all x, x':  P_i(x) and F_i(x, x')  implies  P_(i+1)(x').",
          "section_id": "repo://stdo-v3-generation-rc9/claude/c2cfa9b908d1722ae3f26c32fa4ece691083bdc3/source/standards/FOUNDATIONS.md#FOUNDATIONS%2F4.%20Algebra%2FPreservation%20through%20construction",
          "start_line": 1186
        }
      ],
      "key": "construction_preservation",
      "label": "Preservation through construction"
    },
    {
      "conditions": [],
      "definition": "Distinct accounts of construction: algorithmic account states the required operation; informational account states retained meaning and distinctions; computational binding states how work and observations are realised. Cross-account claims identify maps and preservation obligations.",
      "exceptions": [],
      "grounding": [
        {
          "end_line": 1228,
          "quote": "The algorithmic account states the required operation, the informational\naccount the meaning and distinctions retained, and the computational\nbinding how work and observations are realised\n([§2 Accounts of a construction](#accounts-of-a-construction)). A claim\nacross these accounts identifies its maps and preservation obligations;\nexecution by a person, a machine or a mixed arrangement does not\ndischarge them by itself. A commuting diagram, functor or equivalence\nclaim requires the categorical demonstration above (P1, P6).",
          "section_id": "repo://stdo-v3-generation-rc9/claude/c2cfa9b908d1722ae3f26c32fa4ece691083bdc3/source/standards/FOUNDATIONS.md#FOUNDATIONS%2F4.%20Algebra%2FPreservation%20through%20construction",
          "start_line": 1221
        }
      ],
      "key": "construction_accounts",
      "label": "Accounts and computational binding"
    },
    {
      "conditions": ["Interpretation is conditional on governing basis and assumptions Γ and declared case domain Ω."],
      "definition": "For selected source obligation r, Q_r = interpret(r, Γ, Ω) is the required predicate. An assertion targets q_t and identifies whether it is the full obligation or a narrower claim. Q_r implies q_t makes q_t necessary; q_t implies Q_r makes it sufficient; a stated conjunction q_1 and ... and q_n implying Q_r makes that conjunction sufficient.",
      "exceptions": [],
      "grounding": [
        {
          "end_line": 1244,
          "quote": "For a selected source obligation r, let `Q_r = interpret(r, Γ, Ω)` be its\nrequired predicate over the declared domain Ω of cases. The interpretation\nretains the obligation's scope, conditions, exceptions and permitted\nalternatives. A source identifier or digest authenticates the selected\ntext; it does not prove the interpretation faithful (P1, P3).\n\nAn assertion targets a predicate `q_t` and states whether that predicate\nis the full obligation or a narrower claim. The directions differ:\n\n```text\nQ_r implies q_t                   : q_t is a necessary condition of Q_r.\nq_t implies Q_r                   : establishing q_t is sufficient for Q_r.\n(q_1 and ... and q_n) implies Q_r : the stated conjunction is sufficient.",
          "section_id": "repo://stdo-v3-generation-rc9/claude/c2cfa9b908d1722ae3f26c32fa4ece691083bdc3/source/standards/FOUNDATIONS.md#FOUNDATIONS%2F4.%20Algebra%2FAssertions%20and%20observation",
          "start_line": 1232
        }
      ],
      "key": "obligation_assertion",
      "label": "Source obligation and assertion predicate"
    },
    {
      "conditions": [],
      "definition": "A checker has observation map π: Ω → O and admitted evaluation relation T ⊆ O × R, where R is the core algebra. A deterministic checker is functional; human or probabilistic checkers can admit multiple conclusions for identical observations. sat and fal abbreviate judgments about q_t, not execution states.",
      "exceptions": [],
      "grounding": [
        {
          "end_line": 1258,
          "quote": "Let `π: Ω → O` describe the observations available to a checker and\n`T ⊆ O × R` its admitted evaluation relation, where R is the core result\nalgebra of Results above. A deterministic checker is a functional special\ncase. A human or probabilistic checker can admit different conclusions on\nthe same observations; a universal soundness claim covers all admitted\nconclusions, not a favourable selected run. `indeterminate` is the core\njudgment whose admissible evidence cannot decide the predicate (Results).",
          "section_id": "repo://stdo-v3-generation-rc9/claude/c2cfa9b908d1722ae3f26c32fa4ece691083bdc3/source/standards/FOUNDATIONS.md#FOUNDATIONS%2F4.%20Algebra%2FAssertions%20and%20observation",
          "start_line": 1252
        }
      ],
      "key": "observation_checker",
      "label": "Observation and admitted checker relation"
    },
    {
      "conditions": [],
      "definition": "Assertion provenance records owning obligation, interpretation, assumptions, derivation and shared dependencies. Execution provenance records actual checker, exact subject, applicable environment, inputs, observations and result. Assessment authentication separately binds a result to producer, subject, basis and evidence; it establishes neither provenance.",
      "exceptions": [],
      "grounding": [
        {
          "end_line": 1323,
          "quote": "An expected result or acceptance predicate has assertion provenance: the\nowning obligation, its interpretation, assumptions, derivation and shared\ndependencies, which give it meaning. An execution has execution\nprovenance: the actual checker, exact subject, applicable environment,\ninputs, observations and result. Both are needed for the claims they\nsupport; execution records alone cannot establish that the expected\nanswer follows from the requirement. Authentication of a returned\nassessment, which binds a result to its producer, subject, basis and\nevidence ([EVALUATION §3](EVALUATION.md#3-independent-assessment)), is a\nthird thing and establishes neither provenance (P6, P3).",
          "section_id": "repo://stdo-v3-generation-rc9/claude/c2cfa9b908d1722ae3f26c32fa4ece691083bdc3/source/standards/FOUNDATIONS.md#FOUNDATIONS%2F4.%20Algebra%2FParallel%20derivation%20and%20mutual%20assurance",
          "start_line": 1314
        }
      ],
      "key": "assurance_provenance",
      "label": "Assertion provenance, execution provenance and authentication"
    },
    {
      "conditions": ["Compared constructions or assertions are independently justified within their stated scope."],
      "definition": "Mutual assurance compares constructions or assertions derived along different paths from a common obligation. Requirement-derived assertions check applicable requirement conditions in a realisation; refinement-derived assertions check more detailed obligations. Acceptance and build-level tests are possible examples, not prescribed stages or classes.",
      "exceptions": [],
      "grounding": [
        {
          "end_line": 1312,
          "quote": "Construction and assurance can follow different paths from a common\nobligation. For `requirement → refinement → realisation`, assertions\nderived from the requirement check its applicable conditions in the\nrealisation; assertions derived from the refinement check its more\ndetailed obligations there. Acceptance tests and build-level tests can\ninstantiate these paths\n([ASSURANCE §14](ASSURANCE.md#14-design-to-module-evidence-route)); the\nroles identify the selected sources and subject in this example and\nprescribe no stage names or test classes (P1, P6).",
          "section_id": "repo://stdo-v3-generation-rc9/claude/c2cfa9b908d1722ae3f26c32fa4ece691083bdc3/source/standards/FOUNDATIONS.md#FOUNDATIONS%2F4.%20Algebra%2FParallel%20derivation%20and%20mutual%20assurance",
          "start_line": 1304
        },
        {
          "end_line": 1336,
          "quote": "Mutual assurance compares independently justified constructions or\nassertions within their stated scope. Agreement is compatible with both\nbeing correct and with a shared error; disagreement requires diagnosis",
          "section_id": "repo://stdo-v3-generation-rc9/claude/c2cfa9b908d1722ae3f26c32fa4ece691083bdc3/source/standards/FOUNDATIONS.md#FOUNDATIONS%2F4.%20Algebra%2FParallel%20derivation%20and%20mutual%20assurance",
          "start_line": 1334
        }
      ],
      "key": "mutual_assurance",
      "label": "Parallel derivation and mutual assurance"
    },
    {
      "conditions": ["Quantitative requirements apply where quantified assurance is claimed."],
      "definition": "Risk-governed assurance selected by the actor exercising admitted work authority for stated risk, obligations and resource limits. A quantified model describes its population or uncertainty, priors, likelihoods, shared causes and calibration limits. V denotes a specified violation event, E evidence, and M_i a check missing V under specified evaluation conditions.",
      "exceptions": [],
      "grounding": [
        {
          "end_line": 1370,
          "quote": "Risk model. The actor exercising the admitted work authority selects the required\nassurance for the stated risk, obligations and resource limits. That\nselection identifies material failure consequences, coverage and\nuncertainty, and the conditions for action or acceptance. Additional\nevidence can strengthen a claim only within its applicability and\ndependence account; neither a test count nor an assumed preference for more checks\nsubstitutes for that account (P6, P5).\n\nWhere quantified assurance is claimed, its model states the population or\nuncertainty being modelled, prior information, likelihoods, shared causes\nand calibration limits. For a specified violation event V and evidence\nevent E:",
          "section_id": "repo://stdo-v3-generation-rc9/claude/c2cfa9b908d1722ae3f26c32fa4ece691083bdc3/source/standards/FOUNDATIONS.md#FOUNDATIONS%2F4.%20Algebra%2FQuantified%20assurance",
          "start_line": 1359
        }
      ],
      "key": "quantified_assurance",
      "label": "Quantified assurance and risk model"
    },
    {
      "conditions": [],
      "definition": "Assertion justification explains why a check expresses an obligation; evidence validity identifies dependencies affecting behaviour, conclusion or applicability, including implementation, libraries, data, instruments, environments and assumptions. For change Δ, including a newly invalid assumption, I(Δ) conservatively covers changed facts and affected dependents; D*(t) covers a check's transitive material dependencies in assertion, execution and evidence use; candidate selection S_Δ = {t : D*(t) intersects I(Δ)}.",
      "exceptions": [],
      "grounding": [
        {
          "end_line": 1425,
          "quote": "Assertion justification and evidence validity are related but distinct\ndependency relations. The former explains why a check expresses an\nobligation. The latter identifies what can affect the check's behaviour,\nconclusion or applicability: implementation, shared libraries, data,\ninstruments, environments and governing assumptions. A\nrequirements-to-test link alone is not a complete impact model (P3, P6).\n\nLet Δ be a change to material facts or dependencies, including a newly\ndiscovered invalid assumption. Let `I(Δ)`, the impact cover, conservatively cover the changed\nfacts and their affected dependents. For each check t, let `D*(t)` cover\nthe transitive dependencies material to its assertion, execution and use\nof evidence. A candidate selection is:\n\n```text\nS_Δ = { t : D*(t) intersects I(Δ) }.",
          "section_id": "repo://stdo-v3-generation-rc9/claude/c2cfa9b908d1722ae3f26c32fa4ece691083bdc3/source/standards/FOUNDATIONS.md#FOUNDATIONS%2F4.%20Algebra%2FChange%20impact%20and%20evidence%20reuse",
          "start_line": 1411
        }
      ],
      "key": "change_impact_selection",
      "label": "Validity dependencies and impact selection"
    },
    {
      "conditions": ["Evidence is considered for a new subject and basis."],
      "definition": "The new evidence account is retain_applicable(E_old, new_subject, new_basis) union E_reassessed. Reassessment includes selected checks, new obligations and applicable mandatory gates. Retained evidence keeps its original subject and is used through a new applicability relation; completeness of the combined account differs from freshness of every execution.",
      "exceptions": [],
      "grounding": [
        {
          "end_line": 1447,
          "quote": "For a new subject and basis, let `E_old` be the existing evidence records,\n`E_reassessed` the evidence newly produced by reassessment, and `E_new`\nthe resulting account. Reassessment includes the checks selected in\n`S_Δ`, the assessment of new obligations and the applicable mandatory\ngates. Selection alone produces no execution evidence; failed and\nindeterminate results retain their standing:\n\n```text\nE_new = retain_applicable(E_old, new_subject, new_basis) union E_reassessed.",
          "section_id": "repo://stdo-v3-generation-rc9/claude/c2cfa9b908d1722ae3f26c32fa4ece691083bdc3/source/standards/FOUNDATIONS.md#FOUNDATIONS%2F4.%20Algebra%2FChange%20impact%20and%20evidence%20reuse",
          "start_line": 1439
        }
      ],
      "key": "evidence_reuse",
      "label": "Evidence retention and reassessment"
    },
    {
      "conditions": [],
      "definition": "The candidate's a_c interpretation and projected views represent material obligations and dependencies under its owning Product's constraint-surface fidelity rule. Five support kinds remain distinct: stated formula, discharged mathematical argument, mechanically checked structure, externally assessed predicate, and execution evidence. Source semantics, formal fidelity and actual use retain separate evidence.",
      "exceptions": [],
      "grounding": [
        {
          "end_line": 1506,
          "quote": "The candidate's a_c interpretation represents the material obligations\nand dependency relationships of these subsections under the\nconstraint-surface fidelity rule of the Product that owns this corpus,\nthrough [AXIOMATIC_CALCULUS](AXIOMATIC_CALCULUS.md) and the acquired\nviews that project it. It distinguishes five kinds of support for a statement: a\nstated formula, a discharged mathematical argument, mechanically checked\nstructure, an externally assessed predicate, and evidence from execution.\nAn external predicate retains the source, scope and evidence required to\nestablish it; an arbitrary truth label does not discharge the obligation.\nNo particular proof assistant, probability library or execution engine is\nselected (P1, P6).",
          "section_id": "repo://stdo-v3-generation-rc9/claude/c2cfa9b908d1722ae3f26c32fa4ece691083bdc3/source/standards/FOUNDATIONS.md#FOUNDATIONS%2F4.%20Algebra%2FFormal%20representation",
          "start_line": 1496
        }
      ],
      "key": "formal_representation",
      "label": "Formal representation and support kinds"
    },
    {
      "conditions": [],
      "definition": "Claims carried for falsification, expressly not law: categorical equivalence of 𝒜 and ℐ under some reformulation; universal algorithmic capture of meaning; the statistical Markov boundary with candidate status; and traversal composition laws of any runtime.",
      "exceptions": [],
      "grounding": [
        {
          "end_line": 1536,
